{
  "problem": {
    "title": "Production Planning with Workforce Management and Backorders",
    "description": "A company makes foldable tables over 6 months (Jan-Jun). Each month, it decides how many units to produce in-house, how many to outsource, how many workers to hire or fire, and how much overtime to use. Demand may exceed supply, creating backorders carried to the next month. A 'sold' variable tracks units sold each period (= demand fulfilled + prior backorders cleared). Maximize total net profit = sum(sales_price * sold[t]) minus all costs. Initial workforce: 1000, initial inventory: 15000. Terminal conditions: ending inventory >= 10000, ending backorder = 0.",
    "problem_type": "linear_programming",
    "objective": "maximize",
    "objective_description": "Total net profit: revenue from all units sold minus all production, labor, and inventory costs",
    "constraints": [
      {
        "description": "Workforce balance (init): workforce[jan] = initial_workforce + hire[jan] - fire[jan]"
      },
      {
        "description": "Workforce balance (lag): workforce[t] = workforce[t-1] + hire[t] - fire[t] for t > jan"
      },
      {
        "description": "Sold definition (init): sold[jan] + backlog[jan] = demand[jan] (all demand either sold or backordered)"
      },
      {
        "description": "Sold definition (lag): sold[t] + backlog[t] = demand[t] + backlog[t-1] (current demand plus prior backlog either sold or re-backordered)"
      },
      {
        "description": "Inventory balance (init): inventory[jan] = initial_inventory + produce[jan] + outsource[jan] - sold[jan]"
      },
      {
        "description": "Inventory balance (lag): inventory[t] = inventory[t-1] + produce[t] + outsource[t] - sold[t]"
      },
      {
        "description": "Labor capacity: produce[t] * labor_per_unit <= regular_hours_per_worker * workforce[t] + overtime_hours[t]"
      },
      {
        "description": "Overtime limit: overtime_hours[t] <= max_overtime_per_worker * workforce[t]"
      },
      {
        "description": "Terminal inventory: inventory[jun] >= min_terminal_inventory"
      },
      {
        "description": "Terminal backorder: backlog[jun] = 0"
      }
    ],
    "decision_variables": [
      "produce[t]: in-house production quantity in month t (continuous, >=0)",
      "outsource[t]: outsourced quantity in month t (continuous, >=0)",
      "sold[t]: units sold (demand fulfilled) in month t (continuous, >=0)",
      "inventory[t]: ending inventory at month t (continuous, >=0)",
      "backlog[t]: ending backlog (unfulfilled demand) at month t (continuous, >=0)",
      "workforce[t]: workforce level at end of month t (continuous, >=0)",
      "hire[t]: new hires in month t (continuous, >=0)",
      "fire[t]: firings in month t (continuous, >=0)",
      "overtime_hours[t]: total overtime hours in month t (continuous, >=0)"
    ],
    "parameters": [
      "demand[t]: forecasted demand in month t",
      "initial_workforce: workforce at start of January",
      "initial_inventory: inventory at start of January",
      "sales_price: revenue per unit sold",
      "raw_material_cost: raw material cost per in-house unit",
      "outsource_cost: all-inclusive cost per outsourced unit",
      "holding_cost: inventory holding cost per unit per month",
      "backorder_penalty: backorder cost per unit per month",
      "labor_per_unit: labor hours required per in-house unit",
      "regular_hours_per_worker: regular working hours per worker per month",
      "regular_wage: wage per regular hour",
      "overtime_wage: wage per overtime hour",
      "max_overtime_per_worker: maximum overtime hours per worker per month",
      "hire_cost: cost to hire one worker",
      "fire_cost: cost to fire one worker",
      "min_terminal_inventory: required ending inventory at end of June"
    ],
    "additional_notes": "Key patterns: (1) ORDERED set Months with THREE lag-based equality constraints (workforce_balance, sold_definition, inventory_balance). Each needs a corresponding _init constraint using Months[0]. (2) A sold[t] variable is used as the outflow in the inventory balance — this ensures the inventory balance passes the sign check: inventory(+) + sold(+) - inventory_lag(-) - produce(-) - outsource(-) = 0. (3) Revenue is sum(sales_price * sold[t]) over Months — a variable-based sum, not a parameter constant. (4) Terminal constraints use Months[5] (June = index 5). (5) params.csv is wide-format single-row with all scalar parameters (index_columns=null). (6) Regular wage total = multiply(regular_wage_param, multiply(regular_hours_per_worker_param, workforce[t])) — product of two scalar params then multiply by variable.",
    "csv_file_paths": {
      "sets": "sets.csv",
      "demand": "demand.csv",
      "params": "params.csv"
    }
  },
